3°) there is not at the $\unsure{=|\overline m}$ time a fixed point $\unsure{\widetilde x}$ at infinity --- that is, there is no $L_i$ normalized by $\Gamma$, that is, a splitting of the first type is not at the $\unsure{=|\overline m}$ time of the second (a fact that we established directly, previously).

\margin{This is a specialization of a ``conjectural consequence'' of the conjecture of $\S1$ --- except that one would have to write $\pi^{\Gamma^\circ}=(1)$}

On the other hand, in the case of fixed points at $\infty$, the uniqueness of the image in $X$ means that one and the $\unsure{=|\overline m}$ effective action cannot normalize both an $L_i$ and an $L_j$ ($i\ne j$) --- a fact also established except in the case $(g,\nu)=(0,3)$ --- and uniqueness --- over a fixed $i\in\bar I$ means that the $L_i$ \inserted{($i$ fixed)} normalized by $\Gamma$ is unique, which is a weakening of the relation $L_i=\deleted{\unsure{Z_\pi|Z_{\mathrm{en}}}}\pi^{\Gamma^\circ}$ for these operations, conjectured above.
